# A Guided Laboratory for Self-Consistent Dynamics

A rule can act on a state, but it can also be asked to act on a description of itself. This notebook develops the idea gradually: first through concrete orbits, then through invariant closure, and finally through the diagonal fixed-point theorem. Background on [fixed points](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fixed_point_(mathematics)) and [invariant sets](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Invariant_set) is available through the linked introductions.

## 1. Begin with motion

Choose an affine modular update and trace its orbit. Every displayed state has a finite operational witness: its position in the path.

{{interactive_demo:1}}

{{demo:0}}

<details><summary>Reveal the mathematical definition of reachability</summary>
A state x is finitely reachable from an initial region I under s when x=sⁿ(i) for some i∈I and natural number n. The reachable region is the union of all finite forward images sⁿ[I].
</details>

## 2. Turn an orbit into a region

Instead of following one path, repeatedly enlarge a region by the rule R←I∪s[R]. The highlighted points below are precisely the current approximation.

{{interactive_demo:0}}

{{algorithm:1}}

{{visualization:0}}

<details><summary>Why stabilization gives the least invariant region</summary>
Each stage contains the preceding stage. Any region containing I and closed under s contains every stage by induction. Therefore, when the chain stabilizes, its value is fixed and lies inside every admissible invariant region.
</details>

## 3. Explore the whole transition geometry

The graph view distinguishes the generated region from unreachable states. Compare it with the theory of [reachability in transition systems](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Reachability_problem).

{{visualization:1}}

{{algorithm:0}}

{{demo:1}}

<details><summary>Reveal the finite reachability theorem</summary>
The reachable set contains I and is forward invariant, so it contains the least invariant region. Conversely, the least invariant region contains I and is forward invariant; induction on path length puts every reachable state inside it. The two regions are equal.
</details>

## 4. Put the law into both input slots

The final experiment compares resolving the simulated object first and resolving self-application directly.

{{interactive_demo:2}}

{{algorithm:2}}

{{visualization:2}}

{{demo:2}}

<details><summary>Reveal the fixed-point diagonal argument</summary>
Let H(a) be the least fixed point of b↦U(a,b), and let N be the least fixed point of H. Since N=H(N)=U(N,H(N)), N is diagonal. Conversely, any diagonal fixed point D satisfies H(D)≤D, so the least outer fixed point lies below D. Applying these comparisons to the least diagonal point proves equality.
</details>

## 5. Interpret with care

The selected point is the unique least solution, not necessarily the only solution. Reachability is not attraction, and no numerical physical constant follows without an additional model connecting the semantics to observation. For broader context, compare [domain theory](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Domain_theory) and [dynamical systems](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dynamical_system).

